# DESCRIPTION

Strings are NUL-terminated arrays of `char`, compared as `unsigned char`.

## Memory

`memcpy` copies <n> bytes from <s> to <d>, which must not overlap; `memmove` copies correctly when they do.
`memset` stores the byte <c> in the first <n> bytes of <s>.
`memcmp` compares <n> bytes of <a> and <b>, and returns the difference of the first two that differ, or zero.
`memchr` returns a pointer to the first byte of the first <n> bytes of <s> equal to <c> (converted to an `unsigned char`), or a null pointer.
`memcpy`, `memmove` and `memset` work a word at a time where the machine allows; on rv64 only where source and destination are alike aligned, since a misaligned word load there may trap.

## Strings

`strlen` returns the length of <s>, and `strnlen` the same looking at no more than <n> bytes.
`strcmp` compares two strings and returns the difference of the first two bytes that differ (or of the terminators); `strncmp` looks at no more than <n> bytes.
`strchr` returns a pointer to the first byte of <s> equal to <c>, and `strrchr` to the last; the terminating NUL counts, so `strchr(s, 0)` finds the end.
Both return a null pointer if there is none.
`strcat` appends <s> to the end of <d>, which must have room, and returns <d>.
`strncpy` copies at most <n> bytes of <s> to <d>, padding with NULs to <n>; <d> is not terminated if <s> has <n> bytes or more.

`strdup` returns a copy of <s> in memory from `malloc`, and `strndup` a copy of at most <n> bytes, always terminated.
Each returns a null pointer if there is no memory; the caller frees the copy.

## Error messages

`strerror_r` writes the message for <errnum> into <buffer>, of <size> bytes, truncated and terminated.
It is the XSI form of POSIX: it returns 0 on success, `ERANGE` if the message did not fit, and `EINVAL` for a number `<errno.h>` does not define, for which it writes `Unknown error N` (as far as it fits).
A <size> of 0 writes nothing.

`strerror` returns the same message in a buffer of 40 bytes that belongs to the calling thread and is overwritten by its next call.
The messages are the words of POSIX's descriptions of the errors (`No such file or directory`, `Not supported`), not glibc's.

